winex11: Let the server auto-repeat held keys, so modifier keys repeat too.

MapleStory TW binds skills to Shift / Ctrl / Alt and expects them to fire
again and again while held, exactly as J or any other key does.  On Windows
they do: the keyboard's typematic repeat covers every key, modifiers
included.  Under X they never repeat, whatever `xset r` says, because the X
server refuses in two places (xserver, also Xwayland 24.1):

    _XkbFilterSetState()            xkb/xkbActions.c
        if (filter->keycode == 0) { /* initial press */
            AccessXCancelRepeatKey(xkbi, keycode);

  Soft repeat: AccessXFilterPressEvent() starts the repeat timer for the key
  when its per-key bit is set, and the SetMods/SetGroup action every
  modifier key carries cancels it again on the same press.

    GetKeyboardEvents()             dix/getevents.c
        /* ... or we have a modifier, don't generate a repeat event. */
        if (... || pDev->key->xkbInfo->desc->map->modmap[key_code]) return 0;

  Hard repeat: a second press of a held key that is in the modifier map is
  dropped, so a compositor (gamescope) re-sending the press cannot help.

Measured under gamescope's Xwayland with a passive XI2 listener: J held 2.1s
gives 114 X repeat events, Left Shift held 1.9s gives none, with the Shift
bits of the autorepeat bitmap on for both the core and the slave keyboard.

wineserver already implements Windows-style repeat (server/queue.c, the
desktop's key_repeat: the last key pressed repeats, KeyboardDelay /
KeyboardSpeed from the registry); only winewayland turns it on, because
Wayland sends no repeats.  Turn it on in winex11 as well, and make the server
the only source of repeats: a hardware key-down for a key that is already
down, and not coming from the server's own repeat timer, is dropped.
Otherwise X's repeats and the server's interleave -- with X's delay (600ms
under gamescope by default) longer than the server's (500ms) a held J got a
burst of server repeats at 12ms before X's 40ms repeats took over.

The server only learns that a key is up when the window's thread processes
the KeyRelease, and a busy thread does not.  MapleStory starts a map change
on the Enter that confirms a teleport rock and pumps no messages for a
second or more; the server, still seeing Enter down, queued repeats at 12ms
for a key released long ago, and TranslateMessage made each a '\r' that
opened the chat box on the new map.  The server cannot find out any
earlier: the release may sit unread in the socket, in Xlib's queue (any
XCheckIfEvent for another kind of event, such as the QS_PAINT pass of
UpdateWindow, empties the socket), or with the input method, which gets a
KeyRelease first and forwards it back.  So the repeats are taken back when
the release does arrive:

  - winex11 tells the server as soon as it reads a KeyRelease, before
    XFilterEvent (send_hardware_message with SEND_HWMSG_KEY_RELEASED): the
    server stops repeating that key and removes its repeats that the thread
    has not retrieved yet.  Nothing else changes: no message, no key state.
  - the hardware key-up itself removes them as well, for drivers that do not
    send the notice.

win32u processes driver events before it peeks the server queue (at most
once per 125us tick), so the thread does not get to see a repeat made after
the release.  Repeats the thread had
already retrieved, and injected ones, are left alone.

Injected input (SendInput) is not affected: the repeat
logic only looks at IMO_HARDWARE.

keyrepeat-probe in a separate prefix under gamescope, 2s holds:
                 before           after
    Left Shift   1 WM_KEYDOWN     133, first repeat 501ms, then 12ms
    Ctrl         1                157 (with the first half of this patch)
    Alt          1                161 WM_SYSKEYDOWN, 498ms / 12ms
    J            X's 600/40ms     183, 501ms / 12ms
    Shift, then J held 398ms later: Shift once, then J repeats (as Windows).

busykey-probe on Xvfb, made to look like the game: X autorepeat 600/25,
fcitx5 as XIM server, messages pumped every 16ms, and 1500ms without
pumping after the first WM_KEYDOWN (busy=1 Sleep, busy=2 UpdateWindow every
10ms).  Enter tapped with xdotool for 5..800ms:
                          repeats delivered after the tap
    before                83-84, each with a '\r'
    after                 0 (21 runs, with and without XIM, busy=1 and 2)
    Enter held through the busy period: repeats go on until the release.
Held 2s while pumping, before / after: J 128 / 124, Left Shift 126 / 125,
Right then Shift 84 / 83 for Shift; 500ms then 12ms in every case (the few
missing ones are those queued between the release and its processing).

Two fixes that did not work:
  - repeat only modifiers in the server, leave the rest to X: X's repeats
    of a held arrow key kept cancelling Shift's (+key trace of the game:
    Right held, Shift held, not one Shift repeat), and the other keys fell
    back to X's 600/40ms.
  - skip a repeat while the thread's queue fd is readable (measured with
    the probe above): Xlib empties the
    socket without dispatching the release (busy=2: 84 ghosts again), and
    X's own repeats of the held key make the fd readable most of the time
    (J 127 -> 89, Shift with Right held 85 -> 45).

diff --git a/server/queue.c b/server/queue.c
--- a/server/queue.c
+++ b/server/queue.c
@@ -1885,6 +1885,24 @@
     return 0;
 }
 
+/* remove the auto-repeats of a key that its thread has not retrieved yet; a driver only reports a
+ * release when its thread processes events, and until then the server went on repeating the key */
+static void remove_pending_key_repeats( struct thread_input *input, unsigned int scan )
+{
+    struct message *msg, *next;
+
+    LIST_FOR_EACH_ENTRY_SAFE( msg, next, &input->msg_list, struct message, entry )
+    {
+        struct hardware_msg_data *msg_data = msg->data;
+
+        if (msg->msg != WM_KEYDOWN && msg->msg != WM_SYSKEYDOWN) continue;
+        if (((msg->lparam >> 16) & 0xff) != scan || msg->unique_id) continue;
+        if (!(msg->lparam & (KF_REPEAT << 16)) || msg_data->source.origin != IMO_HARDWARE) continue;
+        list_remove( &msg->entry );
+        free_message( msg );
+    }
+}
+
 /* queue a hardware message into a given thread input */
 static void queue_hardware_message( struct desktop *desktop, struct message *msg, int always_queue )
 {
@@ -1948,6 +1966,9 @@
     if (!always_queue || merge_message( input, msg )) free_message( msg );
     else
     {
+        if ((msg->msg == WM_KEYUP || msg->msg == WM_SYSKEYUP) && desktop->key_repeat.enable &&
+            msg_data->source.origin == IMO_HARDWARE)
+            remove_pending_key_repeats( input, (msg->lparam >> 16) & 0xff );
         msg->unique_id = 0;  /* will be set once we return it to the app */
         list_add_tail( &input->msg_list, &msg->entry );
         set_queue_bits( thread->queue, QS_HARDWARE | msg_bit );
@@ -2326,6 +2347,23 @@
     memset( &desktop->key_repeat.input, 0, sizeof(desktop->key_repeat.input) );
 }
 
+/* a driver has seen the release of a key, and will send the key-up once its thread gets to it */
+static void key_released( struct desktop *desktop, const union hw_input *input )
+{
+    struct thread_input *thread_input = desktop->foreground_input;
+    struct thread *thread;
+
+    if (!desktop->key_repeat.timeout || input->kbd.scan != desktop->key_repeat.input.kbd.scan) return;
+
+    if ((thread = get_window_thread( desktop->key_repeat.win )))
+    {
+        if (thread->queue) thread_input = thread->queue->input;
+        release_object( thread );
+    }
+    if (thread_input) remove_pending_key_repeats( thread_input, input->kbd.scan & 0xff );
+    stop_key_repeat( desktop );
+}
+
 /* queue a hardware message for a keyboard event */
 static int queue_keyboard_message( struct desktop *desktop, user_handle_t win, const union hw_input *input,
                                    unsigned int origin, struct msg_queue *sender, int repeat )
@@ -2369,6 +2407,11 @@
         break;
     }
 
+    /* when the server auto-repeats, it is the only source of repeats: drop the driver's own */
+    if (origin == IMO_HARDWARE && !repeat && desktop->key_repeat.enable &&
+        !(input->kbd.flags & KEYEVENTF_KEYUP) && (desktop_shm->keystate[vkey] & 0x80))
+        return 0;
+
     message_code = (input->kbd.flags & KEYEVENTF_KEYUP) ? WM_KEYUP : WM_KEYDOWN;
     switch (vkey)
     {
@@ -3256,7 +3299,8 @@
         wait = queue_mouse_message( desktop, req->win, &req->input, origin, sender );
         break;
     case INPUT_KEYBOARD:
-        wait = queue_keyboard_message( desktop, req->win, &req->input, origin, sender, 0 );
+        if (req->flags & SEND_HWMSG_KEY_RELEASED) key_released( desktop, &req->input );
+        else wait = queue_keyboard_message( desktop, req->win, &req->input, origin, sender, 0 );
         break;
     case INPUT_HARDWARE:
         queue_custom_hardware_message( desktop, req->win, origin, &req->input );
